## Why This Is Asked
Security guardrails for SQL access are foundational for cloud apps; this question tests practical use of Private Link, Azure Policy, and rollback planning in a beginner-friendly scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Private Endpoint / Private Link
- Azure Policy to forbid public endpoints
- VNet integration for App Service and Functions
- SQL Database publicNetworkAccess and TLS configurations
- Rollback strategy and canary testing

## Code Example
```json
{
  "policyRule": {
    "if": {
      "allOf": [
        {"field": "type", "equals": "Microsoft.Sql/servers"},
        {"field": "Microsoft.Sql/servers/publicNetworkAccess", "equals": "Enabled"}
      ]
    },
    "then": {"effect": "Deny"}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify rollback safety and measure success during the rollout?
- What are potential downsides of private endpoints in a public-facing app, and how would you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of Azure Policy and how to implement guardrails that cover new and existing resources, plus rollout and rollback planning.

## Key Concepts
- Azure Policy and Initiatives
- Deny vs DeployIfNotExists vs AuditIfNotExists
- Storage Account security controls (public access, HTTPS, encryption)
- Tag-based governance and rollout strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
{
  "policyRule": {
    "if": {
      "allOf": [
        { "field": "type", "equals": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts" },
        { "field": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts/publicAccess", "notEquals": "None" },
        { "field": "Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts.enableHttpsTrafficOnly", "notEquals": true },
        { "field": "tags.env", "exists": false }
      ]
    },
    "then": { "effect": "deny" }
  },
  "parameters": {}
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle existing storage accounts that violate these rules during rollout?
- What metrics and alerts would you configure to monitor policy compliance over time?

## Why This Is Asked
Demonstrates ability to implement scalable, workload-driven security without NSG churn, leveraging Azure Firewall Manager, policy-based groups, Private Endpoints, and identity-based controls.

## Key Concepts
- Workload tagging and policy-based groups for dynamic segmentation
- Centralized firewall policies vs. per-VM NSGs
- Private Endpoints for service-to-service traffic
- Managed Identities for authenticating inter-service calls
- Azure Policy to enforce tagging across subscriptions
- Observability via Defender for Cloud and Log Analytics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Azure Policy snippet to require workload-id tag on resources
{
  "policyRule": {
    "if": {
      "allOf": [
        { "field": "type", "in": [
          "Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks",
          "Microsoft.Network/virtualNetworks/subnets"
        ]},
        { "field": "tags['workload-id']", "exists": "false" }
      ]
    },
    "then": { "effect": "deny" }
  },
  "name": "require-workload-id-tag",
  "properties": {
    "displayName": "Require workload-id tag on network scope",
    "policyType": "BuiltIn"
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test rollback and drift if a workload-id tag is accidentally removed?
- What metrics would you surface to confirm zero-trust posture without degrading performance?

